    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters July 6 with a primary objective of capitalizing on favorable matchups against sub-optimal starters. **James Wood** (76.14) is our clear anchor today; hitting lead-off against Burrows provides an elite scoring floor, bolstered by his 0.425 xwOBA and a significant +17% boost from the opposing SP\u2019s peripherals. **Corbin Carroll** (72.02) also presents a high-upside play against Walker Buehler, benefiting from a platoon-neutral dynamic that keeps his projected production in the elite tier despite the park factors. **Isaac Paredes** (68.21) and **Kyle Karros** (63.19) round out our strongest bats; **Kyle Karros** specifically finds himself in a \"plus\" matchup against Eric Lauer, where his +19% SP Skill edge and +8% order bump make him a primary contributor for the day.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the card remains the UTIL spot held by **Austin Riley** (59.29). While his score is respectable, he sits near the threshold of our \"marginal\" category due to an elevated 34% rain risk and a tricky matchup against Freddy Peralta. He narrowly edged out our bench options, as the team is currently managing significant unavailability with **Josh Naylor**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Mickey Moniak** all off-roster for today\u2019s slate. We are keeping a close eye on **Dalton Rushing** (42.48) and **Alex Freeland** (47.03); both are currently pending official lineup confirmation, and their sub-50 scores are heavily reliant on them maintaining their projected spots in the bottom third of the order. If **Dalton Rushing** or **Alex Freeland** are bumped from the starting nine, we will be forced to look toward our deeper bench reserves like **Zach McKinstry** or **Henry Bolte** to maintain our required game caps. Meanwhile, **Jose Caballero** remains our lowest-scoring start (45.22), as he faces a tough path against Jax; his leash is short today given the limited ceiling compared to our core outfielders.",
